      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD) may cause chest pain. The aim was to determine the correlation between ischemia and gastro-esophageal reflux in patients with CAD and to assess the influence of short-term \"anti-reflux\" therapy on the ischemia in patients with GERD and CAD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fifty patients with angiographically proven CAD underwent simultaneous 24-h continuous ECG and esophageal pH monitoring. We assessed the number of ST-segment depression episodes (ST dep.) and total duration of ischemic episodes, expressed as total ischemic burden (TIB). In pH-metry, we assessed: time percentage of pH lower than 4, total time of pH lower than 4 and the number of pathological refluxes (PR). Patients fulfilling the GERD criteria received a 7-day therapy with omeprazole 20 mg bid. On the 7th day of therapy, simultaneous Holter and esophageal pH monitoring was repeated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Total number of 224 PRs in 42 patients (84%) was recorded during esophageal pH-metry. GERD criteria were fulfilled in 23 patients (46%). Out of 218 episodes of ST dep., 45 (20.6%) correlated with PR. GERD patients had larger TIB and higher number of ST dep. (p<0.015 and p<0.035, respectively). The anti-reflux therapy reduced all analyzed parameters of esophageal pH monitoring (p<0.0022) as well as the number of ST dep. (p<0.012) and TIB (p<0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease is common in patients with CAD and may provoke myocardial ischemia. Short-term proton pump inhibitors therapy that restores normal esophageal pH significantly reduces myocardial ischemia, possibly due to elimination of acid-derived esophago-cardiac reflex compromising coronary perfusion-the phenomenon known as \"linked angina\"."
        }
      ]},
